/*
# Segmented

A row of mutually exclusive choices — the control you reach for when the options
are few, named, and worth showing all at once. Its choices are **data**, like
`Menu`'s items and `DataGrid`'s columns:

```declare-fragment
Segmented [ width = 300, value = { app.page },
    choices = { [ ({ id: "controls", label: "Controls" }),
                 ({ id: "table",    label: "Table" }),
                 ({ id: "grid",     label: "DataGrid" }) ] },
    input(v: object) { app.page = "" + v }
    ]
```

### Why data, and not `Segment` children

Because the sliding highlight cannot be built any other way. The pill has to know
which choice is selected and how wide each one is, and a constraint may not ask
the view tree that: `childViews` carries **set membership only**, so `.length` is
live while anything reaching into the collection is refused —
`aggregation over a reactive node collection … derive from data [DECLARE7001]`.
The compiler names the answer.

It is also the mainstream shape. Most native toolkits take segments as titles in an array,
picks from a `ForEach`, Ant Design takes `options`; only Material and WinUI name
the item at all, and both compound it. A bare `Segment` class was the minority
pattern — and bare enough that two apps in this corpus had taken the name for a
paragraph of prose.

### Equal widths — a choice, not a limitation

Segments divide the control's width evenly. This is a DESIGN decision and it
could be otherwise: a `Layout`'s `place()` may aggregate over children — that is
how `SimpleLayout` sizes a variable-width row — so a strategy that placed each
segment by its own label and interpolated the pill between neighbours is
buildable. The `DECLARE7001` refusal binds constraints reaching into the view
tree, not the layout strategy whose job that is. Three reasons not to:

- **The systems that call this a segmented control agree.** The native ones
  divide evenly by default, as does the classic
  segmented `Picker`. The ones that size to content — Bootstrap, Ant — are really
  styling a row of buttons.
- **The segmented convention makes it a content rule**: keep segment labels short and
  similar in length. Equal width enforces that rather than tolerating it. One long
  label makes the whole control look wrong, which is the feedback you want.
- **The sliding pill.** At equal widths it translates at constant width. At
  variable widths it would have to morph its width WHILE travelling, which reads
  as rubbery and worsens the more the labels differ. Most platforms never meet
  this, because their highlight pops.

The cost is real and worth stating plainly: **a `Segmented` does not self-size to
its labels.** Set `width`, or accept `segMin × choices.length`.

### The pill

The highlight springs a NORMALIZED LANE — a float index — not pixels. `x` is then
`lane × segW`, so a resize recomputes `segW` and the pill **snaps** to the right
place instead of skating across the bar on every layout change. Calendar's tab
strip discovered this; it is the one thing the bespoke copies did better than the
library, and it is now here.

The look is the library's: the chosen segment wears a raised `surface` pill with
a hairline. The motion is calendar's. Neither app was wrong about the half it got
right (`chrome-divergences.md` D9).

```declare
App [ width = 340, height = 130, page: string = "one",
    Segmented [ x = 20, y = 20, width = 300, value = { app.page },
        choices = { [ ({ id: "one", label: "One" }),
                     ({ id: "two", label: "Two" }),
                     ({ id: "three", label: "Three" }) ] },
        input(v: object) { app.page = "" + v }
        ],
    Text [ x = 20, y = 74, text = { "showing: " + app.page } ]
    ]
```

## value
The active choice. Bind it to your own state and override `input` to own the
destination, or leave it alone and read `value` back — a `Segmented` works
standalone. Compared against each item's `choice` by identity, so any value type
works.

## isSegmented
**Internal.** The marker a `SegmentedItem` reads to recognize its parent. A
`.declare` class cannot hold a reactive cell without declaring it, so a marker is
necessarily surface — that does not make it API.

## choices
The segments, as **records**: `{ id, label }` or `{ id, icon }` — the same vocabulary
`Menu`'s items use. A choice shows a label **or** an icon, never both. A choice may also
carry a `location`, which makes that segment a real link (see `SegmentedItem.location`).

## input()
**Where the choice goes**, receiving the picked `id`. The default writes `value`;
override it to own the destination.

## pick()
Selects a choice by id, routing through `input`. The seam a segment calls.

## disabled
Disables the whole control. **A segment is not separately disable-able** — a disabled
group leaves the tab order entirely rather than offering unreachable stops.

## segMin
The per-segment width used when you set no `width` on the control. Because a
`Segmented` **does not self-size to its labels**, this is what decides the default
width: `segMin × choices.length`.

## count
How many choices there are, floored at 1 so the width arithmetic never divides by zero.

## segW
One segment's width — the control's width divided by the count. What the pill and every
segment position from.

## idx
Which choice is live, as an index into `choices`, from the **data** — the only place the
question can be asked. `−1` (nothing selected) parks the pill at 0 and hides it rather
than springing to a phantom slot.

## lane
The pill's position as a **float index**, sprung toward `idx`. The whole reason the pill
is correct at any size: `x` is `lane × segW`, so a resize recomputes the width and the
pill **snaps** to the right place instead of skating across the bar on every layout
change.

## segs()
The segment views, for the keyboard rove. A handler-only walk — a constraint may not
aggregate the view tree.

## roveFrom()
Moves keyboard focus to the adjacent segment. Arrows rove; Space or Enter picks where
they land, so **an inactive choice is reachable and selectable entirely from the
keyboard**.

*/

/*
# SegmentedItem

One choice of a `Segmented`, **materialized from that control's `choices` data — never
authored directly**, which is why it carries almost nothing of its own. It is a full
`Control`: its own tab stop, activated by Space or Enter, so a user can tab to an
inactive choice and select it.

It renders rest, hover and press only. **The chosen state is the sliding pill behind
these, not a fill of its own** — which is exactly what lets the pill travel between
segments instead of popping.

## on
Whether this segment is the group's current choice, derived from the parent by identity.

## ink
This segment's text and icon colour, brightening when it is on or hot. One slot, so a
label and an icon can never disagree.

## location
Makes this segment a **real link**: give a choice a `location` and the segment navigates
there in its own `onClick`. This is what keeps a view-switching control visible to link
extraction — the crawler reads `app.location = …` out of activation handlers, and
`input(v)` is not one, since its destination depends on a value that does not exist at
extraction time. Empty for an ordinary segmented control, and an empty read emits no
anchor.

## press()
Delivers this segment's id to the group.

## onClick
Navigates if the choice carries a `location`, then presses.

## onKeyDown
Space and Enter pick; arrows rove to the adjacent segment.

## focusShape()
Rings the segment's pill inset, not its whole box.

## isSegment
**Internal.** The marker a `Segmented` reads to find its own segments — not API.
*/

include [ "control.declare", "icons/icon.declare" ]

class Segmented extends View [
    // the marker (the wrap-probe discipline)

    isSegmented: boolean = true,

    choices: array = [],           // a BARE literal: it owns a cell, so a use-site value wires
    value: object = null,
    disabled: boolean = false,

    // The group honors View's own `replace` (§6 — "fine-grained movement within
    // a place must not bury Back"): with `replace = true`, a pick of a choice
    // that carries a `location` REPLACES the history entry instead of pushing
    // one — the viewer's Reader/Source/Edit, where Back should return to the
    // previous PLACE, never the previous tab. Default false: a switch that is
    // itself navigation (the docs' Guide/Reference) keeps pushing.

    height = 30,
    cornerRadius = 8,
    fill = { theme.control },

    segMin: number = 96,            // per-segment width when the author sets none
    count:  number = { Math.max(1, ((this.choices ?? []) as any).length) },
    width  = { this.count * this.segMin },
    segW:   number = { (this.width - 4) / this.count },

    // Which choice is live, as an INDEX — from the data, which is the only place
    // this question can be asked. −1 (nothing selected) parks the pill at 0 and
    // hides it rather than springing to a phantom slot.

    idx: number = { ((this.choices ?? []) as any).findIndex((c) => c.id === this.value) },

    // ── the delivery seam ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

    input(v: object) { value = v },
    pick(v: object) { input(v) },

    // the member walk (handlers only — the for-of discipline)

    segs() -> array {
        const out = []
        for (const c of this.children) { if ((c as SegmentedItem).isSegment === true) out.push(c) }
        return out
        },

    // arrows ROVE focus across the segments (a focused segment calls this);
    // Space/Enter on the landed segment picks — so an inactive choice is
    // reachable and selectable entirely from the keyboard

    roveFrom(seg: View, d: number) {
        const ss = this.segs()
        const i = ss.indexOf(seg)
        if (i < 0) return
        const n = Math.max(0, Math.min(ss.length - 1, i + d))
        if (n != i) Focus.focus(ss[n] as any)
        },

    // THE PILL. `lane` is a float index the spring drives; `x` multiplies it by
    // the CURRENT segment width, so the pill is correct at any size the moment
    // layout changes and only animates when the selection does.

    lane: number = 0,
    Spring [ attribute = lane, to = { classroot.idx < 0 ? 0 : classroot.idx },
        stiffness = 260, damping = 30, mass = 0.9, epsilon = 0.002 ],

    pill: View [ y = 2, height = { parent.height - 4 }, cornerRadius = 6,
        x = { 2 + (parent as Segmented).lane * (parent as Segmented).segW },
        width = { (parent as Segmented).segW },
        visible = { (parent as Segmented).idx >= 0 },
        fill = { theme.surface },
        stroke = { stroke(1, theme.line) } ],

    // The index rides IN the data — replication has no ordinal of its own, and
    // the pill needs one to place each segment.

    items: Dataset [ contents = { ({ rows: ((classroot.choices ?? []) as any).map((c, i) => ({ ...c, i: i })) }) } ],

    // Replicated as DIRECT children, so a segment's `parent` is the control
    // itself — the pill and the segments are siblings over one cursor.

    datapath = { this.items.value },
    SegmentedItem [ datapath = :rows[] ]
    ]


// One choice. A CONTROL — its own tab stop, Space/Enter activating the pick, so
// the user tabs to an inactive choice and selects it. Materialized from the
// data; not authored directly, which is why it carries no attributes of its own
// beyond what the record gives it.

class SegmentedItem extends Control [
    isSegment: boolean = true,

    height = { parent.height },
    width  = { (parent as Segmented).segW },
    x      = { 2 + :i * (parent as Segmented).segW },

    on: boolean = { (parent as Segmented).value === :id },

    // Rest, hover and press only — the CHOSEN state is the sliding pill behind
    // these, not a fill of their own. That is what lets it travel between them.

    bg: View [ x = 2, y = 2, width = { parent.width - 4 }, height = { parent.height - 4 },
        cornerRadius = 6,
        fill = { parent.on ? null
               : parent.down ? theme.controlPressed
               : parent.hot ? theme.controlHover
               : null } ],

    // A choice shows a LABEL or an ICON, whichever the record carries. Every
    // segmented control in this corpus is one or the other — page switches are
    // labelled, appearance switches are the sun/half-disc/moon — so a segment
    // that showed both would be inventing a case nobody has. The record shape is
    // `Menu`'s, so adding it later is additive.

    ink: Color = { this.on || this.hot ? theme.text : theme.textMuted },

    t: Text [ wrap = false, fontSize = 13, fontWeight = medium,
        visible = { :label != null && :icon == null },
        x = { (parent.width - this.width) / 2 },
        y = { (parent.height - this.height) / 2 },
        textColor = { parent.ink },
        text = { "" + (:label ?? "") } ],

    ico: IconHost [ kind = { "" + (:icon ?? "") }, ink = { parent.ink },
        x = { (parent.width - this.width) / 2 },
        y = { (parent.height - this.height) / 2 } ],

    // The control's own disabled flag follows the GROUP's — a segment is not
    // separately disable-able, and a disabled group takes itself out of the tab
    // order rather than offering unreachable stops.

    disabled = { (parent as Segmented).disabled },

    // A segment MAY be a location. Give a choice a `location` and this segment
    // navigates there itself, in its own `onClick` — which is what makes it
    // visible to link extraction (compiler/src/links.ts reads `app.location = …`
    // out of activation handlers, and `input(v)` is not one; nor could it be,
    // since the destination there depends on `v`, which does not exist at t=0).
    //
    // The docs app is the case that named this: its Guide/Reference switch was
    // the ONLY edge into the reference half of the crawl graph — the class rail
    // is `visible = { mode == "reference" }`, so at the default location its 58
    // links are not in the settled tree at all. When the switch stopped being a
    // link, 58 documents left the static build silently. A second on-page index
    // would be duplicate content; the switch IS the index's front door, so the
    // front door is what had to say where it goes.
    //
    // Empty for every other Segmented in the corpus, and an empty read emits no
    // anchor, so this is inert unless a choice asks for it.

    location: string = { "" + (:location ?? "") },

    press()   { if (!disabled) (this.parent as Segmented).pick(:id) },
    onClick() {
        if (disabled) return
        if (classroot.location != "") {
            // the group's `replace` picks the history verb; the `app.location =`
            // branch stays first so link extraction keeps reading this edge
            if ((this.parent as Segmented).replace) app.follow("#" + classroot.location, true)
            else app.location = classroot.location
            }
        press()
        },

    onKeyDown(e: KeyEvent) {
        if (disabled) return
        if (e.key == "Enter" || e.key == " ") press()
        else if (e.key == "ArrowRight" || e.key == "ArrowDown") (this.parent as Segmented).roveFrom(this, 1)
        else if (e.key == "ArrowLeft" || e.key == "ArrowUp") (this.parent as Segmented).roveFrom(this, -1)
        },

    // the ring hugs the segment's pill, not its whole box

    focusShape() { return { x: 2, y: 2, w: this.width - 4, h: this.height - 4, rad: 6 } }
    ]
